<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">German reunification</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Reunification of Germany</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">German reunification was the process by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> (East Germany) joined the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>) on 3 October 1990, ending the division of the country that had lasted since 1949. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a> and East Germany's first free elections in March 1990, the two states formed a monetary and economic union, and the Two Plus Four Treaty with the four wartime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> restored full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to a united Germany. The date is celebrated as German Unity Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-reunification.jpg" alt="German reunification" width="330" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peer Grimm · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1990-1003-400,_Berlin,_deutsche_Vereinigung,_vor_dem_Reichstag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the unconditional surrender of 1945, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was occupied and divided by the four Allied powers, and no peace treaty followed. The American, British and French zones formed the Federal Republic of Germany on 23 May 1949, and the Soviet zone formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> in October of the same year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1955, and in 1952 the Western Allies and West Germany rejected the Soviet idea of a neutral reunified Germany.</p><p>For decades reunification seemed unlikely. In the 1980s many left-wing parties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, especially the SPD and the Greens, favoured lasting coexistence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> began to falter on 2 May 1989, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> removed its border fence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and thousands of East Germans fled west through Hungary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Peaceful Revolution, a series of protests by East German citizens, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a> on 9 November 1989. On 28 November, Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> announced a ten-point programme for closer cooperation towards eventual reunification. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> held its first free elections on 18 March 1990. A treaty on monetary, economic and social union took effect on 1 July, when the West German Deutsche Mark replaced the East German mark.</p><p>On 23 August 1990 the East German parliament, the Volkskammer, declared the accession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> to the Federal Republic under Article 23 of the West German Basic Law, effective 3 October. On 12 September the two German states and the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> signed the Two Plus Four Treaty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. A treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 14 November confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> as the permanent eastern frontier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The reunited state was not a new one but an enlarged continuation of the Federal Republic, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>'s seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The Two Plus Four Treaty entered into force on 15 March 1991 and ended the remaining limits on German sovereignty, and the last Russian occupation troops left on 31 August 1994.</p><p>The official term is Deutsche Einheit, German unity, and 3 October became the national holiday, German Unity Day; 9 November, the day the Wall fell, was thought unsuitable because it was also the anniversary of the Nazi pogroms of 1938. Not everyone welcomed unification: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> that the United Kingdom did not want it. The economic restructuring of the east was costly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German reunification” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
